From 171a85a6f535a6cb3755895cc38436a7689838c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Besard <tim.besard@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:18:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add cl_pocl_host_kernel: run work-groups on the calling
 thread

A draft extension for the CPU drivers that lets an application run a
kernel's work-groups on its own threads. clCreateHostKernelPOCL prepares a
kernel for one device and local size: it looks up the work-group function
specialized on the local size but generic in grid size and offset, runs
program-scope initializers and records the __local argument sizes.
clRunHostKernelPOCL then runs any range of work-groups of a grid on the
calling thread, synchronously, with no command, event, lock or allocation;
the caller provides the argument values and a scratch area for __local
memory and printf, and the caller's floating-point mode is restored on
return.

This is meant for language runtimes that schedule work themselves, e.g.
KernelAbstractions.jl, for which a one-work-group launch through a command
queue costs several microseconds while the work-group function itself takes
a few nanoseconds.
